  <h1 class="hidetitle">Where Does the Foundation Come From?</h1> 
  <h2>AI can compress, but not replace</h2> 
  <h3>Ed Solovey</h3> 
  <p>AI-assisted development is now the default expectation for new software engineers, but the productivity it delivers depends on professional judgment, and AI is taking over exactly the work that used to develop that judgment. Drawing on two vantage points—teaching software engineering at Boston University and leading an internship program at Digits, an AI-forward financial technology (fintech) startup—in this article I trace what happened when new graduates were handed the same agentic tooling as senior engineers and why the fix was a phased on ramp that compressed a decades-long career arc into weeks. For engineers with grounding, AI is a multiplier; without it, AI creates a productivity illusion in which output outpaces understanding. The article argues that universities and companies share one obligation: Embrace AI in education and onboarding, but introduce it in an intentional sequence, and assess the understanding polished output cannot prove, so the foundation gets built rather than bypassed.</p> 
  <h3>The Multiplier</h3>
  <p>I have been a software engineer for more than 20 years and can undoubtedly say that I am a more productive engineer with agentic AI in the loop. In areas that I am already an expert, I am at least two to three times more productive, and agentic AI has also enabled me to venture into areas that I normally would not have contributed to.</p> 
  <p>However, the reason I am able to get this boost is that I can lean on my 20 years of experience for a foundational understanding of what is happening, can stay in planning mode for hours asking the right questions, and can critically inspect the generated code—spotting shortcomings and raising the right follow-up questions.</p> 
  <p>Fair or not, the expectation is that recent graduates are also more productive as a result of the capability of these new tools. The challenge is: Where, how, and when do they get the foundation that I have built up over my career to navigate their work?</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Two Vantage Points</h3>
  <p>I write this from two vantage points: (1) For the past two years I have been teaching software engineering at Boston University; and (2) I am leading a summer internship program at Digits, an AI-forward financial technology (fintech) startup. From the classroom, I see what a four-year program can plausibly teach. From the internship, I see what the work actually demands of graduates who walk into a codebase already assuming the productivity gains AI is supposed to deliver.</p> 
  <p>Students entering college this fall will graduate into a workforce where employers already assume them to be more productive, because of AI tooling, than the engineers being hired today. How should universities prepare them for that? And how should software companies onboard their new hires?</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>The Deep End</h3>
  <p>To set the stage, let’s acknowledge, much as we did with the interns at Digits, that we are in a brave new world, and no one knows yet what the best way is to ramp up into an AI-forward software engineering company. Not only are the ideas brand new, but the development processes, expectations, and safeguards are evolving every day. It is an intimidating and moving target.</p> 
  <p>When the new grads arrived at Digits for the summer internship, our first instinct was to bring them straight to where the full-time engineers already are. We had recently reshaped our development environment around agentic AI, adding virtualized sandboxes where the model operates and skill-based workflows that wrapped well-defined tasks such as writing a new service or producing an architecture document—the whole stack of patterns that lets a Digits engineer hold 10 threads at once.&nbsp;It felt natural to hand the same tools to the interns.</p> 
  <p>So, on day one, we exposed them to container environments, skills around architecture-document generation, and code-exploration agents that could walk an unfamiliar repository and explain it back. We explained how various parts of this ecosystem work and encouraged them to experiment.</p> 
  <p>Historically at Digits, even pre-GenAI, before anyone could make a nontrivial change to a service, they wrote a one- to three-page architecture document. The doc covers the standard concerns—what we are building and why, what the dependencies are, how it will be tested, how it will be deployed, what the rollback plan looks like. It is the artifact that aligns the team before code gets written. Experienced Digits engineers can produce such a document in about a day, because they know what to include and, more importantly, what to leave out.</p> 
  <p>When augmenting the dev environment with AI, we built a skill that walks you through producing one of these docs. It prompts you through the standard sections, gathers context from the codebase, drafts each section, and lets you revise. For experienced engineers, the skill is a real productivity boost—it removes about half of the boilerplate work and leaves them free to focus on the genuinely load-bearing design decisions. We expected the interns to get the same lift.</p> 
  <p>The interns had used the skill, and they had produced architecture documents that were technically complete. Every section was filled, every reference to the existing code correct, every prompt answered, but they were unreadable by humans.</p> 
  <p>The summary sections, each of which should have been three sentences, were instead three paragraphs long. The dependency sections cataloged every import statement of every adjacent file. The test plans rehearsed the entire conceptual testing landscape rather than naming the two or three things that actually had to be tested for the specific change. Reading the documents felt like reading transcripts from someone learning the topic in realtime. Which, in a sense, was exactly what they were doing.</p> 
  <p>Without solid, foundational grounding, how were the interns supposed to know what the right level of abstraction is and where to cut details out?</p> 
  <p>The interns had not failed the skill. The skill had asked them, at each step, what should go in this section, and they had answered as well as they could. The problem was that they had no judgment about what each section should contain—what level of detail fit the audience, what was relevant versus what was scaffolding, what could be left out because the reader could be trusted to know it. The skill had been built by engineers who had that judgment to begin with. It assumed the user could direct it. Without that direction, the skill produced everything it could, and the output was useless—not because the content was wrong—but because there was too much of it to read.</p> 
  <p>We had handed the most abstract tools to the people least equipped to direct them. And the symptom on the surface—verbose, unreadable architecture docs—looked like a tool problem. But it was not; it was a grounding problem. The interns were drowning in information. They had no mental model to anchor anything to, no vocabulary for what the code was doing, no instinct for which questions were the right questions to ask the agent in the first place. Giving them more powerful tools didn’t help them. It was making it worse, because each tool produced output they then had no basis to evaluate.</p> 
  <p>So, we adjusted our approach.</p> 
  <p>We moved to a phased on ramp. Each phase was deliberately brief—measured in days, not weeks—because these were incredibly bright, recent graduates, and we knew that the ability to pick up new ideas and concepts was not the problem. The problem was introducing the concepts in a way that did not allow understanding to build on top of other layers of understanding.</p> 
  <h4>First Phase</h4>
  <p>The first phase was manual coding: closed environment, no agentic assistance, write the code by hand against the existing codebase. The point was not to prove the interns could do it—most of them already could—but to build the vocabulary: to let them name the components and the conventions and the language patterns of the codebase out of their own typing, so that when an AI agent later produced output that referenced those same things, they would recognize them instead of having to parse them from scratch.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h4>Second Phase</h4>
  <p>The second phase was AI-as-assistant. We do a lot of paired programming at Digits, and this phase involved engineers using AI much as they would interact with someone they are pairing with. They could now use the model in narrow and targeted ways: Ask it to explain a function, walk through a control flow, summarize what a particular service did, suggest a single-method implementation.</p> 
  <p>The point was to build the mental map of the codebase, with AI as a tutor rather than a co-author. By the end of this phase, the interns could navigate the system, talk about it in the team’s vocabulary, and form their own positions before the agent had a chance to suggest one.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h4>Third Phase</h4>
  <p>Only then did we move to the agentic phase. By the time our interns got there, when the agent produced an architecture document, they could read it and say: This paragraph is doing too much, this section is the actual contested decision, this dependency is wrong. They had what the skill had assumed they had to begin with—direction.</p> 
  <p>What we discovered, in walking the program back, was that we had reinvented something old by accident. The three-phase on ramp at Digits is a sped-up version of what used to happen across a career. An engineer in the 2000s started by writing code by hand because there was no other way. They got assistance—IDE autocompletion, then language servers, then early code generation—gradually, as they were ready to direct it. By the time they were more experienced, they could orchestrate sophisticated tools, because they had built the foundation underneath. The arc happened over years, mostly by accident, through whatever combination of grunt work and luck the early career happened to provide.</p> 
  <p>We compressed that arc into weeks. The shape was the same. And that fact is worth sitting with for a moment, because it implies something about where the foundation actually comes from—and about what we are doing, with AI in the loop everywhere, to the conditions under which it gets built.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Foundational Layers</h3>
  <p>The first realization, looking back at the program, was that we had not skipped any phase. We had compressed each one. The interns still had to write code unassisted before they could meaningfully direct an agent. They still had to use AI as a tutor before they could use it as a co-author. That ordering is not a coincidence. Each phase builds something the next phase needs in order to be intelligible—vocabulary, then a mental map of the codebase, then the ability to read agentic output critically.</p> 
  <p>AI is the accelerant <i>within</i> each phase, not a substitute for the phases themselves. This also clarifies what <i>foundation</i> really means here. It is not only syntax, algorithms, or the ability to solve a small programming problem without help, though those still matter. The foundation that AI most depends on is professional judgment: knowing what question is actually being asked, what level of detail belongs in an architecture document, what has to be tested, what can fail in deployment, what would need to be monitored after release, and what can be left out because it is not load-bearing. Much of that judgment was once absorbed indirectly through the work itself. But now AI is good at doing exactly the kinds of surface work through which that judgment was often built.</p> 
  <p>You could argue that engineers have always learned by borrowing from examples, copying code, and adapting what they found. That is true, but the old version still forced contact with the details. You had to integrate the code, debug it, reshape it, and discover why it did or did not fit. AI can remove more of that contact, which is why the sequence matters.</p> 
  <p>Historically, some of that pressure was built into the work by accident. If you wrote code by hand, the size of a change was naturally limited by the time and attention it took to produce it. If you wrote tests by hand, you had to decide which behaviors mattered enough to protect. If you wrote an architecture document yourself, you had to choose what belonged in the document and what could be left out. Those activities were slow, but they were valuable, because they forced the engineer to notice, choose, and explain. AI can remove the slowness, which is useful, but to avoid losing the benefits of the process while picking up the speed, the surrounding structure still has to preserve the acts of noticing, choosing, and explaining. In a company, that structure is onboarding, code review, architecture review, and deployment discipline. In an academic course, it is sequence and assessment.</p> 
  <p>This is the asymmetry at the center of the whole problem. For an engineer with grounding, AI is a productivity multiplier. It lets them move faster because they can still direct the work, notice when the output is wrong, and decide what deserves attention. For an engineer without that grounding or for an engineer whose grounding is no longer being exercised by the surrounding structure, AI can create a productivity illusion. The work gets larger, faster, and more polished, but the understanding underneath does not necessarily expand with it.</p> 
  <p>That is the pressure this puts on academia. Graduates will be expected to use AI, and AI really can accelerate work. But it can only accelerate a layer of understanding that already exists.</p> 
  <p>The educational question, then, is how to expose students to AI in ways that accelerate each layer without short-circuiting the foundation their later work will depend on.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>Academia</h3>
  <p>The wrong answer is to try to keep AI out of the classroom. Students will reach for it immediately, and after graduation they will be expected to use it. Used well, it is a real accelerant. But that only makes system thinking more important, not less, because system thinking is what lets foundational layers build faster without collapsing. A student who can reason about a system as a whole—how state moves, where responsibilities sit, what can fail, and how parts compose—can compress the path. A student who outsources that reasoning too early cannot.</p> 
  <p>So, the academic challenge is not to tell students simply not to use AI. It is to design curricula that make it clear why students should not want AI to mediate every difficult encounter they have, at least not at first, and why they should not want AI to interfere with them internalizing the system-level reasoning that later leverage depends on. The goal is not to withhold the accelerant. It is to introduce it in a way that speeds each layer of learning without replacing the work of building that layer.</p> 
  <p>In practice, that means designing courses around sequence. Students should first have to form a view of the problem, struggle enough to discover where their understanding is thin, and build some working mental model of the system in front of them. Only then should AI enter as leverage: to help compare approaches, surface edge cases, explore unfamiliar parts of a codebase, or accelerate implementation once the underlying reasoning belongs to the student. The curriculum should reward that order explicitly, so that what gets optimized is not mere output, but the student’s capacity to understand, direct, and critique the output.</p> 
  <p>The question, of course, is what forces that sequence. The answer is assessment. If students can succeed by submitting polished output alone, many will use AI to bypass the very struggle that builds the foundation. So, courses have to evaluate the things polished output cannot prove: whether students can frame the problem, explain the system, justify the design, describe how it should be tested, and respond when a follow-up question exposes a gap. The point is not to forbid AI. It is to make genuine understanding the only reliable path to doing well.</p> 
  <h4>Evolution of Assessment</h4>
  <p>That points to a different style of assessment than the field has often relied on. The instinctive response to AI is to retreat to the kinds of evaluations it cannot easily touch: closed-book exams, tightly proctored coding tasks, assignments narrow enough to make outside help harder to use. There may still be a place for some of those, just as there is still a place for doing arithmetic by hand before reaching for a calculator. If they become the center of the curriculum, however, they defend the wrong thing. The goal is not to prove that students can perform in an AI-free vacuum. The goal is to know whether they can think clearly in a world where AI is present.</p> 
  <p>That means leaning harder on assessment formats that make understanding visible. Open-ended projects force students to make choices rather than recover a known answer. Design reviews force them to justify those choices in front of another human being. Oral exams make it obvious very quickly whether students actually understand the system they are describing or are just paraphrasing generated output. Staged deliverables help, too: a problem framing, then a design, then a testing plan, then the implementation, with feedback in between. Each checkpoint asks for a kind of understanding that has to exist before the next kind of leverage makes sense.</p> 
  <p>What these formats share is that they do not treat output as sufficient evidence of learning. A polished submission may still matter, but it is not enough on its own. The assessment has to reveal whether the student can explain what the system is doing, identify what could fail, choose what should be tested, and respond coherently when the situation changes. Those are the habits AI will amplify after graduation, but only if the classroom first makes them unavoidable.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h4>Assessments by Example</h4>
  <p>Here we take a detailed view at the motivation behind a potential assessment structure for two courses: Introduction to Software Engineering (freshmen/sophomores) and Software Engineering in Practice (juniors/seniors).</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <p><b>Introduction to Software Engineering</b></p>
  <br /> 
  <p>Consider an Intro to Software Engineering course covering the following topics: assembly, basic C memory management, and object-oriented programming with C++. The intended audience is students who have had little or no prior software engineering experience.</p> 
  <p>At first glance, that may sound like a strange response to AI. If the world is moving toward higher-level agentic tools, why spend precious course time on low-level programming? But that is exactly the point. The more fluent the tools become at producing plausible surface-level code, the more important it is that students have at least one course where software is not allowed to collapse into text. They need to see that programs execute on machines, move through memory, fail in concrete ways, and eventually have to be organized as systems.</p> 
  <p>Let’s assume that the class starts by spending three weeks on computer architecture, basics of RAM, registers, and the CPU as well as assembly. An assignment on this part of the course might look like this:</p> 
  <p>• <b>Overview</b>. Students are given the specification for a minimal instruction set, one that has been covered in class, and asked to write 10 programs at increasing levels of complexity. For example, the first program might be to move the value at memory location 34 to register 2, while the last program might be to multiply the contents of memory location 17 by the contents of memory location 18 by using conditional loops.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Philosophy</b>. No GenAI should be used for this assignment. The intent is not to teach students how to code in assembly because that is a skill that will be useful for them after graduation. The intent is to spend a minimal amount of time on building a mental awareness of how a computer works at a very basic level so that subsequent layers can build on this foundation.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Evaluation.</b> A five-minute, heavily rubric-based conversation with each student should be given by either the instructor or a teaching assistant. Questions during the conversation might include, “Please explain to me, line by line, how your solution for problem 8 works,” or “Describe to me how a program would read values at 15 adjacent memory locations.”</p> 
  <p>After that, let’s assume the class spends three or four weeks on C, with particular attention to pointers, arrays, strings, structs, allocation, freeing, and the kinds of bugs that come from managing memory directly. An assignment on this part of the course might look like this:</p> 
  <p>• <b>Overview</b>. Students are asked to implement a small C library with explicit ownership rules. For example, they might build a dynamically resizing array of strings that supports creating the collection, appending a copied string, removing an entry, looking up an entry, resizing the backing storage, and destroying the collection. The assignment would require them to use <code>malloc</code>, <code>realloc</code>, and <code>free</code> directly, handle allocation failures, and provide a small test program that exercises both normal behavior and failure cases.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Philosophy</b>: Limited GenAI use is allowed but only to ask questions about existing C libraries and not about code-generation aspects for the assignment. The intent is not to make students spend weeks memorizing C library trivia. The intent is to make memory visible. Students need to experience the fact that data has a lifetime, that two pointers can refer to the same allocation, that returning a pointer creates obligations, and that a program can appear to work while leaking memory or depending on undefined behavior. AI can help explain compiler errors or suggest additional tests, but it should not replace the student’s own reasoning about who owns each allocation and where it gets released.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Evaluation.</b> The submitted code should be run against functional tests and a memory checker, but that should not be the whole assessment. Students should also submit the ownership plan they wrote before implementation and a short debugging note describing one memory issue they encountered or one memory issue their tests are intended to catch. The same five-minute rubric-based conversation can then ask questions such as, “Which function owns the memory for this string after <code>append</code> returns?”, “What happens if <code>realloc</code> fails here?”, or “Show me where every allocation made by this library is eventually freed.” The goal is to assess whether the student understands the memory model, not merely whether the final program happens to pass.</p> 
  <p>Finally, let’s assume the class spends the rest of the semester on object-oriented programming in C++, including classes, interfaces, encapsulation, resource acquisition is initialization (RAII), composition, and tests. An assignment on this part of the course might look like this:</p> 
  <p>• <b>Overview</b>. Students are asked to build a nontrivial C++ application large enough to require several modules. For example, they might build a file-backed task manager, a small search index, a command-line game, or another application with parsing, state, persistence, error handling, tests, and user-visible behavior. The application should be decomposed into clear components such as a domain model, parser, storage layer, command handler, and reporting or presentation layer. Roughly 80 percent of those modules should be written without GenAI code generation. For one designated module, students are encouraged to use GenAI in planning mode to evolve the module’s scope, discuss design alternatives, identify edge cases, and then generate a first implementation of that single module.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Philosophy</b>. This is the first assignment where GenAI code generation is intentionally part of the work, but it is contained. The intent is to give students real exposure to AI-assisted implementation and to satisfy the legitimate curiosity they have about how this process works without letting the whole system become an opaque generated artifact. Most of the application still has to come from the student’s own design and implementation. The AI-generated module then becomes something they have to integrate, test, adapt, and understand in relation to the rest of the system. That balance matters. Students should experience both sides: the confidence that comes from building most of the system themselves and the responsibility that comes from owning code they did not initially write.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Evaluation.</b> Assessment should focus most heavily on the modules where GenAI code generation is not expected. Students should be asked to explain every line of code in those modules, why the module boundaries are where they are, how the design evolved, and what tradeoffs they made as the application grew. The AI-assisted module should still be assessed, but differently: Students should submit the planning transcript or a summary of the planning process, identify what the model generated, describe what they changed, and explain how they tested and integrated it. In the rubric-based conversation, questions might include, “Walk me through the control flow from this command to this stored result,” “Why does this class own this state?”, “What changed between your first design and final implementation?”, or “What did the generated module get wrong that you had to correct?” The goal is for students to see GenAI code generation as a tool they can direct and critique, not as a substitute for understanding the system.</p> 
  <p>This is the same layered understanding sequencing argument from the Digits on ramp, but in course form. The assembly assignment builds the student’s ability to reason about execution. The memory assignment builds their ability to reason about lifetime and failure. The C++ application assignment builds their ability to reason about a system.</p> 
  <p>AI access expands across those assignments because the student’s ability to direct it is expanding. In the first assignment, too much AI would replace the trace. In the second, it can help expose mistakes, but the ownership reasoning must belong to the student. In the third, it can generate one bounded module, but the larger design judgment has to be assessed in human terms.</p> 
  <p>That is what makes the sequence defensible in an AI-forward curriculum. It is not nostalgia for low-level programming. It is a way of making foundations visible before they are abstracted away. Students leave not merely having written some C and C++, but having experienced three different levels at which software has to make sense: the machine, the memory, and the system. Those are exactly the layers they will need later when AI produces code that looks plausible but has to be understood, tested, and owned by a human engineer.</p> 
  <p><b>Software Engineering in Practice</b></p>
  <br /> 
  <p>A junior- or senior-level course should look different. By this point, students should already have basic programming fluency. The course should therefore spend less time asking whether they can implement a tightly specified function and more time asking whether they can reason through an underspecified system. This is where open-ended assignments become essential. In professional software engineering, the hard part is often not writing code for a known specification. The hard part is discovering what the specification should have been.</p> 
  <p>One assignment might be a design-first project with an intentionally incomplete prompt:</p> 
  <p>• <b>Overview</b>. Students are given a product-level request rather than a detailed engineering specification. For example: “Build a system that lets instructors collect, review, and comment on student project submissions.” The prompt should leave important questions unanswered: Who are the users? What states can a submission be in? What permissions matter? What should happen if a student resubmits? What parts of the system need to be durable? Students use GenAI heavily in planning mode to explore these questions, generate alternatives, identify edge cases, and pressure-test assumptions. The deliverable is not a full implementation. It is a short architecture document: problem framing, assumptions, user flows, data model, major components, risks, testing strategy, and open questions.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Philosophy</b>. The point is to make students practice the work that comes before implementation. AI is useful here precisely because it can widen the planning conversation. It can ask about edge cases the student missed, compare possible architectures, or help turn vague product language into concrete engineering choices. But the students still have to decide. They have to separate useful suggestions from irrelevant ones, notice when the model is inventing requirements, and produce a design that reflects their own judgment. This builds system-level thinking, because the student is not only solving a problem inside a system, but also deciding what the system is.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Evaluation:</b> Assessment should focus on the design conversation—the grade should depend on whether the students can defend the resulting design. Questions might include, “Which requirements did you infer, and which did you leave open?”, “What alternative architecture did you reject and why?”, “Where is the riskiest dependency?”, “What state transition is most likely to create a bug?”, or “Which test would give you the most confidence that this system works?” The goal is to assess whether the student can turn ambiguity into a coherent technical plan.</p> 
  <p>A second assignment might ask students to implement a meaningful change inside an existing codebase:</p> 
  <p>• <b>Overview</b>. Students are given a moderately sized application and an open-ended change request. For example, they might add role-based permissions, introduce audit logging, support a new import format, or change the persistence model for part of the application. The request should be specific enough to be actionable, but not so specific that the design is already solved. Students are expected to use GenAI in planning mode before writing code—to explore the codebase, identify the likely touchpoints, compare implementation strategies, and draft a test plan. They may also use GenAI to generate some implementation code, but only after submitting their own change plan.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Philosophy</b>. This assignment moves the students closer to the professional setting. Most software work happens in existing systems, where every change has a blast radius. The important skill is not just to produce a patch. It is understanding what the patch touches, what assumptions it changes, and what could break somewhere else. Planning-mode AI can accelerate that exploration but only if the student has enough system understanding to ask useful questions and reject shallow answers. The assignment should therefore reward time spent in planning, not treat planning as a prelude to the “real” work.</p> 
  <p>• <b>Evaluation.</b> The final submission should include the implementation, the tests, the original change plan, and a short note explaining how the plan changed during implementation. In the rubric-based conversation, students should be asked to trace the change through the system: “Where does this request enter the application?”, “Which modules did you choose not to touch?”, “What would break if this assumption changed?”, “Which test protects the most important behavior?”, or “What did AI suggest that you decided not to use?” The assessment is not whether the students used AI impressively. It is whether they can explain the system-level consequences of the change they made.</p> 
  <p>The advanced course therefore applies a similar comprehension-building sequence in a different direction. While the introductory course uses constraints to build foundations, the advanced course uses ambiguity to test whether those foundations have become judgment. AI is more present because the student is now ready to use it in the mode professional engineers increasingly need—not merely as a code generator, but as a planning partner whose output has to be questioned, narrowed, and turned into a design the human can defend.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h4>Scalability of Assessment</h4> 
  <p>The obvious objection to this model is scale. A five-minute conversation with every student sounds reasonable in a seminar and impossible in a large introductory course. If the assessment model is simply added on top of the old one, that objection is correct. There is not enough bandwidth to keep the traditional closed-book exam structure, manually grade those exams, review programming submissions, and run oral assessment checkpoints.</p> 
  <p>But that is not the model being proposed. The shift is a substitution. If the course is explicitly moving away from closed-book exams as the central evidence of learning, then the time that would have been spent writing, administering, and manually grading those exams can be redirected toward conversations with students. Teaching assistants are not being asked to do an additional category of work on top of everything else. They are being asked to spend more of their assessment time in direct technical conversation with students and less of it assigning partial credit to handwritten exam answers.</p> 
  <p>That is a better use of a teaching assistant’s time. It gives students feedback from a human who can press on their actual understanding, and it gives the TA practice doing something much closer to real software engineering judgment: reading code, asking why it was written that way, identifying gaps in reasoning, and distinguishing a superficial answer from a grounded one. The TA is not merely a grading machine. The TA becomes part of the instructional system that helps students learn how to explain and defend technical work.</p> 
  <p>The second objection is fairness. If TAs are having these conversations, how do we keep the assessment from becoming subjective? The answer is not to pretend that human judgment disappears. The answer is to make that judgment narrow, explicit, and calibrated. The rubrics have to be detailed enough that the conversation is not an open-ended impression of whether a student “seems to understand.” For the assembly assignment, a rubric might specify what counts as correctly tracing register state, explaining a branch, or identifying where a value is read from memory. For the C assignment, it might specify what counts as explaining ownership, handling allocation failure, or identifying the consequence of a missing <code>free</code>. For the C++ application, it might specify what counts as explaining module boundaries, control flow, design evolution, and the integration of the AI-generated module.</p> 
  <p>That kind of rubric also changes the instructor’s role. If, as the instructor, I am no longer spending that time manually grading closed-book exams, I can spend it preparing the TAs for these conversations, running calibration sessions, reviewing sample answers, spot-checking borderline cases, and stepping in when a student’s score is ambiguous. The goal is not that every TA independently invent a standard, but that the course staff shares one standard and applies it consistently.</p> 
  <p>This still requires care. It requires scheduling, training, and rubrics that are specific enough to be useful. It is not obviously less scalable than the assessment structures we already accept. It is a different allocation of the same instructional bandwidth, aimed at the thing we now most need to measure: whether students can understand, explain, and own the work in front of them.</p> 
  <p>&nbsp;</p> 
  <h3>One Diagnosis, Two Audiences</h3>
  <p>The Digits story and the classroom story are not two separate arguments. They are the same argument at two different points in the pipeline. At Digits, the missing foundation showed up after graduation, when interns were handed tools that assumed they already had the judgment to direct them. In the classroom, the danger is that students can now use AI to produce work that looks complete before that judgment has formed. In both cases, the failure mode is the same: Output becomes detached from understanding.</p> 
  <p>Once framed this way, the responsibility is shared. Universities cannot treat AI as something to ban until graduation, because students will graduate into workplaces where it is already part of the workflow. But neither can companies treat AI as something that magically converts new graduates into experienced engineers. Both sides have to design for the same progression: first vocabulary, then mental models, then judgment, then leverage.</p> 
  <p>That also means the answer is not to declare traditional education obsolete and replace it with industry apprenticeship. The Digits on ramp worked because the interns arrived with something to ramp from: enough programming fluency, enough comfort with unfamiliar problems, enough system intuition to build on. At the same time, the classroom needs the signal from industry to know what kind of judgment graduates will be expected to exercise. Academia and industry do not replace each other here. They have to meet in the middle of the same progression.</p> 
  <p>For academia, that means building courses where students encounter difficulty before AI smooths it away, and where assessment makes understanding visible. For companies, it means onboarding new engineers through phases that let them learn the codebase, vocabulary, and judgment their tools assume. In both settings, the goal is not to slow students or new hires down for its own sake. The goal is to make acceleration meaningful.</p> 
  <p>Where does the foundation come from? It comes from the parts of learning and work that AI should not erase too early: tracing the system by hand, explaining why a design holds together, debugging the failure you did not expect, defending a decision to another human being, and gradually earning the right to hand more of the surface work to the machine. AI can compress that path. It cannot replace the path.</p> 
